# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_RESOURCE_CREATE: parent | options << 32 */
int64_t resource_create(uint64_t parent_options, uint64_t base, uint64_t size,
                        const char *name, uint64_t name_size, uint32_t *out);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A resource is the authority for privileged operations over a range of one kind of space.
Calls that touch hardware or a privileged service take a handle to a resource that grants what they touch: vmo_create_physical(2) a range of MMIO, interrupt_create(2) an IRQ, ioports_request(2) I/O ports, smc_call(2) a function id, and others a system resource.
Resources only narrow: a process holding one makes smaller ones with `resource_create`, and hands them out.
The first process receives the root resource, which grants every kind.

The kinds are:

| Kind | Value | The range |
| `CROI_RSRC_KIND_MMIO` | 0 | physical addresses |
| `CROI_RSRC_KIND_IRQ` | 1 | interrupt numbers |
| `CROI_RSRC_KIND_IOPORT` | 2 | I/O ports (amd64 only) |
| `CROI_RSRC_KIND_ROOT` | 3 | the root resource; it cannot be created |
| `CROI_RSRC_KIND_SMC` | 4 | SMCCC function ids (arm64 only) |
| `CROI_RSRC_KIND_SYSTEM` | 5 | a system service, <base> its number and <size> 1 |

A resource of size 0 is its kind's ranged root: it grants any range its kind allows.
The system resources, by <base>:

| Base | Name | Gates |
| 1 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_VMEX_BASE` | vmo_replace_as_executable |
| 3 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_INFO_BASE` | the CPU and kernel memory statistics of object_get_info |
| 4 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_CPU_BASE` | job_set_cpu_reservation |
| 5 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_POWER_BASE` | system_powerctl, cpu_set_idle_states |
| 8 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_IOMMU_BASE` | iommu_create |
| 10 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_PROFILE_BASE` | profile_create |
| 11 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_MSI_BASE` | msi_allocate |
| 14 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_TRACING_BASE` | trace_configure, pmu_configure sampling |
| 17 | `CROI_RSRC_SYSTEM_MEMORY_BASE` | account_create |

The others (0, 2, 6, 7, 13, 15) can be made but no call checks them.

## resource_create

`resource_create` writes to <out> a handle to a new resource that is a part of the resource <parent>.
The first argument packs the parent handle (low 32 bits; it needs WRITE) and the options (high 32): the kind in the low 16 bits and `CROI_RSRC_FLAG_EXCLUSIVE` (0x10000) above it.
The new resource covers [<base>, <base> + <size>) of the kind; <size> must not be 0.
<name> and <name_size> give a name, at most 31 bytes.
The handle has TRANSFER, DUPLICATE, INSPECT, WRITE and GET_PROPERTY.

The parent must grant the range, else `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`.
Physical memory (RAM and firmware's), the registers the kernel drives itself, and on amd64 the I/O ports it uses cannot be granted by any resource, the root included.
On arm64 neither can the PSCI calls that change CPU or system state, which are the kernel's.
A parent that is not a ranged root and is exclusive, or an exclusive child of one, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
An exclusive resource may not overlap another resource of the same kind, nor may another overlap it; if it would, the call fails with `CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND`.
A range outside the kind's space is `CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND`, and a kind this architecture lacks is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.
An unknown kind, a flag other than EXCLUSIVE, the kind ROOT, a base and size that wrap, and a size of 0 are `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

The kind, flags, range and name of a resource are read with object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_RESOURCE`.

